Distance-estimated 3D fractals (technique references)
-----------------------------------------------------
The HYPERSPACE style's five distance estimators in
app/src/main/res/raw/hyperspace_frag.glsl are implemented from the published
mathematics of these constructions. No source is copied from any of the works
below; they are listed because the constructions are theirs and the shader
would not exist without them.

  Kaleidoscopic IFS (the TEMPLE estimator) - introduced by "Knighty" on
  fractalforums.com (2010): fold space with absolute values and conditional
  reflections, scale about an offset, and rotate between iterations.
  Documented at http://blog.hvidtfeldts.net/index.php/category/kaleidoscopic-ifs/

  The Mandelbox (the JEWEL estimator) - Tom Lowe (2010): a box fold and a
  sphere fold followed by a scale, with the derivative carried alongside as a
  scalar so the escape radius can be turned into a distance estimate.

  Apollonian / Kleinian limit sets (the GASKET and CORAL estimators) - the
  classical construction: the limit set of the group generated by translations
  of a cell and inversion in a sphere. The floored inversion that keeps the
  CORAL variant connected rather than shattering it into a dust follows the
  "pseudo-Kleinian" family, also due to Knighty.

  The Mandelbulb (the BULB estimator) - Daniel White and Paul Nylander (2009):
  z -> z^n + c with the power taken in spherical coordinates. The estimate is
  the Douady-Hubbard potential, log(r) * r / dr.

  Distance-estimated rendering generally, and the orbit-trap colouring used
  here, follow the write-ups by Mikael Hvidtfeldt Christensen (Syntopia,
  http://blog.hvidtfeldts.net) and Inigo Quilez (https://iquilezles.org).

The HYPERSPACE style's own contributions - the bank of independently living,
independently rotating bodies with bounding-sphere culling and per-body frames,
the five-act story law driven by the track's macro-dynamics, the two-trap
background filigree, the aura/rim/translucency shading and the camera - are
this project's.

